>
  <div class="container section">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#6b5a54;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/products/">Products</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/products/dried-chillies/">Dried Chillies</a> &rsaquo; Erjingtiao
    </nav>
    <h1>Erjingtiao Chilli</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A slender, aromatic Sichuan chilli at roughly 15,000–30,000 SHU — the flavour backbone of
      hotpot, chilli oil and doubanjiang. Bought for fragrance more than raw heat.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Erjingtiao (二荆条) is the aroma variety, not the heat variety. At roughly 15,000–30,000 SHU it sits at the top of the medium band, but its real value is fragrance — which is why it is used whole or coarse-crushed in hotpot and doubanjiang rather than milled to fine powder.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What is Erjingtiao chilli?</h2>
    <p>
      Erjingtiao is a slender, wrinkled Sichuan chilli whose name translates loosely as
      "two-thorn strip", after the pods' characteristic taper. It sits at roughly 15,000–30,000 SHU
      — the top of the medium band into high-medium — and is grown across the Sichuan basin. Unlike
      the colour varieties (Jinta) or the heat varieties (Sanying, Jixin), Erjingtiao is chosen for
      <strong>aroma</strong>.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Heat (SHU)</strong></td><td>15,000 – 30,000 (upper medium)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Region</strong></td><td>Sichuan (Chengdu plain)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Fruit type</strong></td><td>Slender, wrinkled, thin-fleshed pod</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Hotpot, doubanjiang, chilli oil, paste</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="data-note">
      SHU is a representative range (MEDIUM confidence) and varies by Sichuan growing region — some
      sources place Erjingtiao at 10,000–20,000. Confirm your target window with us.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What is Erjingtiao used for?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Hotpot and mala soup base</li>
      <li>Doubanjiang (fermented broad bean paste)</li>
      <li>Chilli oil and infused oil</li>
      <li>Fermented chilli paste and seasoning</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Why aroma fades in fine powder</h2>
    <p>
      Erjingtiao's fragrance comes from a thin flesh and a high surface-to-volume ratio — the same
      geometry that makes it aromatic also makes it fragile. Grinding to fine powder exposes more
      aromatic surface to air, so the volatile fragrance fades faster than in a thick-fleshed
      variety. This is why Erjingtiao is traditionally used <strong>whole or coarse-crushed</strong>:
      a 20–40 mesh crush holds its aroma far longer than an 80-mesh powder. If your application
      demands fine powder, accept a trade-off in aroma and consider blending a thick-fleshed variety
      to carry the heat while Erjingtiao carries the flavour.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Erjingtiao vs Jinta vs Xian</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Variety</th><th>SHU</th><th>Bought for</th><th>Best form</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Erjingtiao</strong></td><td>15,000 – 30,000</td><td>Aroma, fragrance</td><td>Whole, coarse crush, paste</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Jinta</strong></td><td>10,000 – 20,000</td><td>Colour, glossy appearance</td><td>Visible flakes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Xian</strong></td><td>10,000 – 20,000</td><td>Versatile dark red</td><td>Powder, sauce</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The decision rule: judge what the finished product is scored on. If it is scored on fragrance
      — a hotpot base or doubanjiang — Erjingtiao wins. If it is scored on how the flake looks,
      Jinta wins. If you need an all-round red powder, Xian is the versatile middle.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What most guides skip about Erjingtiao</h2>
    <p>
      Most guides say Erjingtiao is "fragrant" and move on. What they skip is the processing
      implication: because the aroma is volatile and the flesh is thin, <strong>grind level is a
      flavour decision, not just a texture decision</strong>. The same Erjingtiao at 20–40 mesh and
      at 80 mesh will deliver visibly different aroma in the finished product. Specify mesh as a
      flavour spec, and ask for whole pods if the aroma must survive long supply chains.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How to specify Erjingtiao</h2>
    <p>
      Name the variety, the form (whole, coarse crush or paste-grade), target SHU window, and mesh.
      For aroma-sensitive applications, favour whole or 20–40 mesh crush and confirm moisture under
      13%. If the product is a fine powder, tell us — we will advise on blending to hold both heat
      and fragrance.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is Erjingtiao chilli?</h3>
      <p>Erjingtiao (二荆条) is a slender, aromatic Sichuan chilli at roughly 15,000–30,000 SHU — the flavour backbone of hotpot, chilli oil and doubanjiang. It is bought for aroma more than raw heat.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>How hot is Erjingtiao?</h3>
      <p>Erjingtiao sits at the top of the medium band into high-medium — roughly 15,000–30,000 SHU, about 15–30x hotter than sweet paprika and below the high-heat Sanying and Zidantou band.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is Erjingtiao used for?</h3>
      <p>Erjingtiao is the classic for Sichuan applications: hotpot base, doubanjiang, chilli oil and fermented chilli paste. Its aroma — not heat — is the point, so it is often used whole or coarse-crushed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Why does Erjingtiao aroma fade in fine powder?</h3>
      <p>Its aroma comes from thin flesh and a high surface-to-volume ratio. Fine grinding exposes more aromatic surface to air, so fragrance fades faster — whole or coarse-crushed Erjingtiao holds aroma better than 80-mesh powder.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Erjingtiao vs Jinta vs Xian — which do I choose?</h3>
      <p>Jinta for colour-and-appearance flakes, Erjingtiao for aroma (paste and hotpot), Xian for a versatile red powder. If aroma is the point, choose Erjingtiao; if looks, Jinta.</p>
    </div>

